{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuntD4muqwHLkArscgJP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not really. When you deploy, Cloudflare restarts every Durable Object instance that moves to the new version. There's no setting that keeps an instance on old code for a set time. In-flight requests get a short grace period to finish, but open connections such as WebSockets are dropped, and clients must reconnect.\n\nThe closest option is **gradual deployments**. Use `wrangler versions upload`, then `wrangler versions deploy` to split traffic by percentage (for example, 90% old and 10% new). Each Durable Object ID is consistently assigned to one version, so some objects keep running old code while you ramp up. Any object that moves to the new version still restarts at that moment.\n\nFor anything stateful, plan for restarts. Persist state to storage rather than memory, and add client reconnect logic. The WebSocket Hibernation API also helps, since it can make deploys less disruptive than holding sockets in memory."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2258,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2258},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afbaa523-631d-41d0-945e-4a8537da1841","uuid":"9333ff33-ca82-4b00-bee5-58449c4e093a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:08:08.594Z","request_id":"req_011CfuntCrNafefFtHNDDuxh"}
